Based on the three screenshots and your description, what is happening is:

– When you show the “spy-glass” window you first clip and then translate the canvas so that everything drawn by the Executor is shifted up and to the right of the mouse.  
– Non-rotated text labels end up in the right place because `drawImageOrLabel(…, transform = undefined)` simply calls `context.drawImage(…)` and respects the existing canvas translation.  
– As soon as you have a non‐zero rotation, in `Executor.replayImageOrLabel_` we hand `drawImageOrLabel` a non‐null “canvasTransform” matrix, and under the hood that routine does a full `context.setTransform(…)`, wiping out the translate that was put in place by the spy-glass clipping.  Result: your green “3” spins in place at the map’s original pixel origin, instead of at the spy-glass window.

The simplest fix is to stop letting `drawImageOrLabel` reset the entire transform when there is a rotation.  Instead, manually apply only the rotation matrix on top of whatever translation is already in the context, draw the image/label, and then restore.  Concretely, in `src/ol/render/canvas/Executor.js` change the block around the `drawImageOrLabel` call as follows:

